\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces The river analogy of \S  \ref  {sec:river}, drawn. Each panel is a stretch of the frequency grid: towns are grid points, leaves are photons, the current is the velocity coupling $\Gamma $, and the muddiness of the water is the opacity $\hat  \chi $. \emph  {Top:} in an expanding flow the current runs one way (high $\nu $ to low $\nu $, left to right), so each town asks the upstream neighbour it has already visited. \emph  {Middle:} in a compression zone the current reverses; the old solver still asks upstream and gets the wrong leaf count, while the signed coupling also makes the muddiness negative. \emph  {Bottom:} the Knop solver asks downstream, using the previous iteration's stored intensities, and the muddiness depends only on $|\Gamma |$.}}{9}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:river}{{4}{9}{The river analogy of \S \ref {sec:river}, drawn. Each panel is a stretch of the frequency grid: towns are grid points, leaves are photons, the current is the velocity coupling $\Gamma $, and the muddiness of the water is the opacity $\hat \chi $. \emph {Top:} in an expanding flow the current runs one way (high $\nu $ to low $\nu $, left to right), so each town asks the upstream neighbour it has already visited. \emph {Middle:} in a compression zone the current reverses; the old solver still asks upstream and gets the wrong leaf count, while the signed coupling also makes the muddiness negative. \emph {Bottom:} the Knop solver asks downstream, using the previous iteration's stored intensities, and the muddiness depends only on $|\Gamma |$}{figure.4}{}}
